  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe strategy leverages convex relaxation and alternating minimization to address the non-convexity of the Erd\u0151s problem. By allowing h to take continuous values and projecting back to binary, we can escape local optima and find better solutions. This approach combines convex optimization with thresholding to maintain feasibility.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe relax h to be continuous in [0,1], then solve the convex problem to minimize the maximum overlap. Define variables:\n\n- h \u2208 [0,1]^n_points\n- t \u2208 \u211d: threshold for binarization\n\nObjective:\nmin_t max_{k} [\u03a3 h_i (1 - h_{i+k}) dx]\n\nSubject to:\n\u03a3 h_i dx = 1\n\nOperations:\n- Convex optimization solves for h\n- Thresholding binarizes h: h_bin = (h \u2265 t)\n- Adjust t to enforce \u03a3 h_bin dx = 1\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n1. Initialization\n   a. Start with initial_h_values as continuous h\n   b. Set dx = 2/n_points\n   c. Initialize t = median(h)\n\n2. Main loop\n   a. For each iteration:\n      i. Optimize h in [0,1] to minimize max overlap\n      ii. Compute h_bin by thresholding\n      iii. Adjust t to maintain sum constraint\n      iv. Update h to h_bin for next iteration\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Start with multiple initial_t (median, mean, quantiles)\n- Maximum iterations: 100\n- Tolerance: 1e-6\n- cvxpy for convex optimization\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Each iteration: O(n_points^2) for correlation\n- Robust to local optima through convex relaxation\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Check integral constraint and h \u2208 [0,1]\n- Recompute C5 via evaluator",
